content.text:
The agent splits the work. **Code decides what to offer and when to close; the AI reads the supplier and writes the words.** Each round goes through the same pipeline, and each step uses a well-known negotiation tactic.

## How it runs

### 1. Before the session opens: read the supplier
[dossier.py](negotiation-arena-kit/starter-kit/arena_kit/dossier.py) gathers everything the data says about each supplier:
- call and meeting notes, letters;
- past negotiations (what it asked for vs what it settled at);
- its finances and credit score;
- how promptly we pay it;
- scorecards, the contract, the part's cost drivers.

It also flags cash signals, e.g. "asked us to pay within 10 days".

[buyer.py](negotiation-arena-kit/starter-kit/arena_kit/agents/buyer.py) then makes **one AI call per supplier** that turns this into a profile:
- **persona** (e.g. "owner of a family foundry, values partnership");
- **tone** to use;
- **pressures** on the supplier;
- **what it values** beyond price;
- **levers**: which terms matter to it;
- **what to avoid**;
- **which data arguments will land**;
- **questions** to ask.

This happens before the clock starts, so it costs no negotiating time.

### 2. Every round: code decides the move
[strategy.py](negotiation-arena-kit/starter-kit/arena_kit/strategy.py) works in **C**, the case's own total-cost measure: price plus payment terms, freight, quality, uncovered need, and supplier health in strategic cases.

1. **Estimate the supplier's floor.** In order of preference: its own cost breakdown, the low end of our should-cost estimate, or 10% under its opening price.
2. **Pick a target C for this round:**
   - an ambitious opening, by case goal (5–12% under their price, or "no increase at all" for a price-increase letter), never below the supplier's estimated floor;
   - each later round concedes a shrinking share of the remaining gap;
   - if the supplier didn't move, we move only half a step;
   - we never concede more than the supplier just did;
   - never anything worse than our alternative.
3. **Build up to 3 term sheets that all cost us that target:**
   - **our terms:** everything the way we want it, at a higher price;
   - **their terms:** their terms, improved wherever a change helps both sides, at a lower price;
   - **a trade:** one term moved their way (e.g. faster payment), for at least 1% off the price.
4. **Decide whether to close.** Accept when:
   - the supplier agrees;
   - its final offer beats our alternative;
   - its offer meets our target and it has stopped conceding;
   - or rounds run out.

   Walk away if nothing beats the alternative.

### 3. Every round: choose the arguments
[claims.py](negotiation-arena-kit/starter-kit/arena_kit/claims.py) computes each data argument the way the arena checks it:
- index changes;
- the notice period the letter gave, against the contract's;
- delivery or defect record;
- a rival's offer;
- should-cost;
- demand forecast.

It drops arguments that would help the supplier, never reuses an ID, skips anything previously contradicted, and ranks the rest by the profile first, then by strength.

### 4. Every round: the AI writes, code checks
The AI gets a curated briefing:
- the profile;
- the supplier's last email in full;
- how our earlier arguments checked out;
- what our options trade;
- the ranked arguments and candidate questions.

It returns only the soft parts: salutation, an opening that answers the supplier, which arguments to use, a bridge sentence, one question, a close.

[writing.py](negotiation-arena-kit/starter-kit/arena_kit/writing.py) then assembles the email. It inserts the arguments word for word and writes the options paragraph from the actual term sheets. It deletes any sentence that leaks urgency or a budget, contains a data ID, or uses a number not in the source material. If the AI is slow or fails, code sends a plain email that still carries the best arguments.

### 5. RFQs
All suppliers get their emails at the same moment, so no thread times out. Laggards more than 25% above the best are dropped after two rounds.

## How this steers the AI

The AI is never asked "what should we offer?" It is steered in four ways:
- **Narrowed input:** it sees a briefing chosen for this moment, not the whole data dump or our internal limits.
- **Pre-made choices:** it picks from checked arguments and suggested questions rather than inventing them.
- **Fixed output shape:** it fills slots in a JSON template, so it can't add prices or term sheets.
- **Checked output:** code rewrites the numbers and removes risky sentences.

So the AI's influence is what it's good at: reading the person across the table, answering their point, choosing which arguments fit, and setting the tone.

## The negotiation tactics in the code

| Tactic | Where | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| **Anchoring** | opening target; our preferred package listed first | An ambitious first offer pulls the final price toward us. |
| **Several equal-cost offers at once** | 3 term sheets at the same cost to us | Which one the supplier picks reveals what it values, without us giving anything away. |
| **Trading across terms** | "a trade" and "their terms" sheets | Give what's cheap for us and valuable to them (payment speed, start date, contract length). Never give without getting. Take gains for both sides for free. |
| **Shrinking concessions** | concession curve | Each step is smaller than the last, which signals we're nearing our limit. |
| **Reciprocity** | half step if they don't move; never more than they gave | Concessions are earned, not handed out. |
| **Knowing our walk-away point** | cap at our alternative; walk away | We never accept or offer anything worse than what we'd get without a deal. |
| **Objective criteria** | checked data arguments | Argue from facts the supplier can verify (indices, contract notice), not demands. |
| **Gathering information** | questions; cost-breakdown request | A cost breakdown sharpens our floor estimate for later rounds. |
| **Competition** | RFQ: cite the best rival offer; target 1% under it | Other bidders become leverage. |
| **Relationship management** | profile tone; supplier-health awareness | Partnership tone for a family firm; avoid squeezing a strategic supplier into losses. |
| **Information discipline** | leak filter | Never reveal urgency, a budget, or a missing alternative. |

## Where it's still weak

The numbers behind these tactics are hand-picked constants: how ambitious to open, the shape of the concession curve, the floor safety margin. The P01 run showed they matter: an overrated cost floor made the agent accept too early. That's why the next step is running all 10 practice cases, then making those numbers adapt to each supplier's history and behaviour.

If you want to share this with your team or use it for the jury pitch, I can turn it into a page.

